//@ expect: accept
//@ reason: `this` used as a plain function-call ARGUMENT (`useSelf(this)`,
//@ reason: not `return this;`) is a new capability (Chapter 18 Step 37) --
//@ reason: previously `this` was only handled in the `return this;` position;
//@ reason: everywhere else it silently emitted a raw `this` pointer where a
//@ reason: smart pointer was expected, a generated-C++ compile error (the
//@ reason: worst failure mode for a compiler promising "if it compiles, it's
//@ reason: safe" -- neither EscapeHatchChecker nor DanglingChecker rejected
//@ reason: it at the CeeLing level; g++ was the only thing that ever caught it).
//@ reason: Safe for the same reason `return this;` already is: every CeeLing
//@ reason: object is compiler-wrapped in a smart pointer, so passing `this`
//@ reason: lowers to shared_from_this() -- a genuine additional owning/shared
//@ reason: reference to the SAME control block, never a raw alias into
//@ reason: something that could already be destroyed. Requires
//@ reason: SmartPointerAnalyzer::methodPassesThisAsArgument's new detection
//@ reason: scan to fire (forcing enable_shared_from_this + a shared
//@ reason: construction root for Node) -- this fixture's own runtime
//@ reason: assertion below is what actually proves the whole pipeline works
//@ reason: end to end, not just that it compiles.
//@ run: 9

class Node {
public:
    int v;
    Node(int val) { v = val; }
    int useSelf(Node other) {
        return other.v;
    }
    int chainSelf() {
        return useSelf(this);
    }
};

int main() {
    Node n = new Node(9);
    std::cout << n.chainSelf() << std::endl;
    return 0;
}
